    "knowledge_point": "Integrating a General Exponential Function With a Linear Exponent",
    "problem": "$\\int {5}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x=$",
    "worked_solution": "If we let $u=-x$, then \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-1\\,⟹\\,-\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {5}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {5}^{u}\\cdot (-1)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\int {5}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{{5}^{u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{{5}^{-x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
